{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfpmGw8x1QKpFgLXG3SEQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 5 tests pass.\n\n`src/str.js:7`:\n```js\nfunction truncate(str, n) {\n  if (str.length <= n) return str;\n  return str.slice(0, n) + '…';\n}\n```\n\nUses U+2026 `…`, the single ellipsis character, not three dots.\n\nTwo limitations I left in rather than guessing at:\n\n- **No surrogate-pair safety.** `.length` and `.slice` count UTF-16 code units, so truncating mid-emoji (e.g. `truncate('👍👍👍', 3)`) splits a pair and yields a replacement glyph. Fixing it means deciding what \"n characters\" means — code points, or grapheme clusters (`'é'` as `e` + combining accent, flags, skin-tone modifiers). That's a real behavior choice, not a detail, so I didn't pick for you. If you want it, `[...str]` handles code points cheaply; graphemes need `Intl.Segmenter`.\n- **No input validation.** A non-string `str` throws on `.length`; a negative `n` returns just `…`; `undefined` for `n` makes `<=` false and slices to `0`. Matches the existing `trim`, which also assumes a string.\n\nUnrelated: `ISSUE.md` in this repo asks for a `slugify` function. That's a different task and I didn't touch it — mentioning it only in case you meant to point me there."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":325,"cache_read_input_tokens":20887,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":325},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"13bf2add-0ea5-43eb-bc8e-e37474605ad9","uuid":"1004bf50-caac-4895-a16e-28e1120eefd8","timestamp":"2026-10-08T12:24:04.880Z","request_id":"req_011CfpmGvuoauRPGUK4CSBLJ"}
